course: dictionary
lesson: 1900
title: Color or colour?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The same word can do different jobs. Color can be a noun, as in «a bright color», a verb, as in «to color a picture», or an adjective, as in «color photos». The rest of the sentence tells you which job it is.
explain: Spelling depends on where you live. «American spelling dropped the u from words like "color" and "favorite"», so Americans write color, while Britain, Canada, Australia, New Zealand and Ireland write colour. Both spellings are correct in their own country, so do not mark a classmate wrong for using the other one.
explain: Read the clues to find the meaning. «The story has lots of color» means the story is lively and interesting, not that it has paint on it. «Her anger colored her judgment» means her anger changed the way she saw things. Ask yourself: does this fit a crayon, or does it fit a feeling?
fact: Color means what we see when light of different kinds reaches our eyes, such as red, blue, green or yellow.
fact: To color something means to fill it in with crayons, pens or paint.
fact: «Colors» can also mean the flag of a team, school or army.
fact: «With flying colors» means very well, and «show your true colors» means showing what you are really like.
fact: The American spelling is color; British, Canadian, Australian, New Zealand and Irish spelling is colour.
exercise: «The children colored a picture of the rainbow.» What does colored mean here? | To sing loudly | To win a race | *To fill in with crayons or paint | To hide under water
exercise: Both «color» and «colour» are correct. Which spelling is used in Britain? | *colour | color | Both, always | Neither
exercise: «The story has lots of color.» What does color mean here? | *Interest and liveliness | Red and blue paint | Dark and gloomy | A team flag
test: What does colored mean in «Her anger colored her judgment»? | Covered in dust | Painted with bright paint | Made it smaller | *Changed the way she saw things
test: Which job does color do in «color photos»? | *Adjective | Verb | Noun | Adverb
exam: Which statement is true? | Spelling never changes between countries | Colour is a mistake everywhere | Only color is ever correct | *Both spellings are correct, each in its own country
